# Multi-Tenant Database Research
1. Introduction
Multi-tenancy is an architecture where one application serves multiple customers or organizations called tenants. Each tenant's data must be properly separated and protected from other tenants.
There are three common database approaches for multi-tenant applications.

2. Approach 1 — Shared Database / Shared Schema
All tenants use the same database and the same tables.
Example:
Database
 ├── organizations
 ├── users
 ├── employees
 └── departments
Each tenant-specific record contains a tenant_id or organization_id.
Example:
users
--------------------------------
id | tenant_id | name
1  | 101       | User A
2  | 102       | User B
3  | 101       | User C
Advantages
•	Lowest infrastructure cost.
•	Easier to create and manage.
•	Simple database deployment.
•	Efficient use of database resources.
•	Suitable for a large number of small tenants.
Disadvantages
•	Strong application-level tenant isolation is required.
•	A missing tenant filter can expose another tenant's data.
•	Database queries and indexes can become large.
•	Backup and restore of a single tenant can be more difficult.
________________________________________
3. Approach 2 — Shared Database / Separate Schema
All tenants use the same PostgreSQL database, but each tenant has a separate database schema.
Example:
Database
 ├── tenant_a
 │    ├── users
 │    └── employees
 │
 ├── tenant_b
 │    ├── users
 │    └── employees
 │
 └── tenant_c
      ├── users
      └── employees
Advantages
•	Better data isolation than a shared schema.
•	Tenant data is separated at the schema level.
•	Easier to manage tenant-specific database objects.
•	Can provide better isolation without requiring a separate database for every tenant.
Disadvantages
•	More complex than a shared schema.
•	A large number of schemas can become difficult to manage.
•	Database migrations must be applied across multiple tenant schemas.
•	Connection and schema management require additional application logic.
________________________________________
4. Approach 3 — Separate Database
Each tenant has its own database.
Example:
Tenant A → Database A
Tenant B → Database B
Tenant C → Database C
Advantages
•	Strongest database-level isolation.
•	Each tenant can be backed up and restored independently.
•	Tenant-specific database configuration is possible.
•	A problem in one tenant's database is less likely to directly affect another tenant.
Disadvantages
•	Highest infrastructure cost.
•	More databases must be monitored and maintained.
•	Database provisioning and migrations become more complex.
•	Managing a very large number of databases can be difficult.
________________________________________
