6. Technical Reasoning
Cost
Shared schema has the lowest cost because all tenants share the same database and tables.
Separate schema requires additional schema management but still shares the same database infrastructure.
Separate databases have the highest cost because each tenant requires a separate database environment or database instance.
Isolation
Shared schema depends heavily on correct tenant_id filtering and application-level controls.
Separate schemas provide stronger logical separation because each tenant's tables exist in a different schema.
Separate databases provide the strongest database-level isolation because tenants do not share the same database.
Scalability
Shared schema can efficiently support many tenants because all tenants use the same tables and database resources.
Separate schema can scale well, but managing thousands of schemas can increase operational complexity.
Separate databases provide strong isolation and independent scaling, but managing many databases increases infrastructure and operational overhead.
Complexity
Shared schema is the simplest approach to implement.
Separate schema requires schema provisioning, schema selection, and migration management.
Separate database requires database provisioning, connection management, backups, monitoring, and migrations for many databases.
Maintenance
Shared schema is generally easiest to maintain because there is one database structure.
Separate schema requires database changes to be applied across multiple schemas.
Separate database requires database changes, monitoring, backups, and maintenance across multiple databases.

